WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
The first film followed stored food energy through respiration.

2
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.180
What makes that process speed up or slow down?

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.420
Keep the same pea-cell pathway on screen.

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.780
We will change one condition at a time.

5
00:00:14.783 --> 00:00:18.903
The rate of respiration means how fast cells carry out the process.

6
00:00:20.203 --> 00:00:24.503
We can track oxygen entering or carbon dioxide leaving over time.

7
00:00:26.123 --> 00:00:30.583
Faster motion through the pathway raises both the rate gauge and the output counter.

8
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:32.873
Start with temperature.

9
00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:37.893
In a suitable range, warmer cells usually respire faster.

10
00:00:39.193 --> 00:00:44.633
The pathway blocks move more quickly, so oxygen use and carbon dioxide output rise.

11
00:00:45.933 --> 00:00:48.073
This does not mean every increase helps.

12
00:00:49.167 --> 00:00:51.247
Suppose the cells keep getting hotter.

13
00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:54.187
Must the rate keep rising forever?

14
00:00:55.547 --> 00:00:58.347
Follow the pathway in your mind, then make your prediction.

15
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.300
No. Too much heat damages cells, and respiration slows or stops.

16
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.020
The curve bends downward in the damaged range.

17
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.740
Boiling is an extreme case.

18
00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:21.420
It kills the pea cells instead of making them respire faster.

19
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:24.073
Next, change oxygen.

20
00:01:25.533 --> 00:01:28.973
Close the oxygen valve, and fewer blue dots reach the pathway.

21
00:01:30.413 --> 00:01:32.433
Respiration that uses oxygen slows.

22
00:01:34.053 --> 00:01:37.053
Seal the valve, and the pathway runs on the oxygen left.

23
00:01:37.053 --> 00:01:42.653
As that oxygen runs out, respiration with oxygen slows, then stops.

24
00:01:43.733 --> 00:01:49.533
Now change sugar. With too little glucose, the pathway lacks fuel and slows.

25
00:01:51.093 --> 00:01:52.573
Add some, and the rate can rise.

26
00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:56.553
Keep adding, and another condition becomes limiting.

27
00:01:57.993 --> 00:02:03.053
In yeast, a lot of sugar can increase fermentation while respiration with oxygen

28
00:02:03.053 --> 00:02:04.053
falls.

29
00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:06.893
That yeast branch changes how we measure.

30
00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:10.933
Fermentation also gives off carbon dioxide.

31
00:02:12.453 --> 00:02:14.953
Baker's yeast ferments even when air is present.

32
00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:19.593
So most gas in a yeast balloon can come from fermentation.

33
00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:24.993
Oxygen use is cleaner for measuring its respiration with oxygen.

34
00:02:26.133 --> 00:02:30.113
Each control acts on the same pathway, but the pattern has limits.

35
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:33.913
Temperature helps only within a suitable range.

36
00:02:35.313 --> 00:02:38.533
Oxygen matters to respiration that uses oxygen.

37
00:02:39.853 --> 00:02:43.393
More sugar helps only until something else holds the rate back.

38
00:02:44.383 --> 00:02:50.003
Respiration releases stored food energy; temperature, oxygen, and sugar change its

39
00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.003
rate. The picture gives the pattern.

40
00:02:54.243 --> 00:02:58.303
Real measurements must show whether the pattern holds and where its limits are.

41
00:02:59.317 --> 00:03:04.677
The film has not shown the real pea ranges, yeast values, units, or source limits.

42
00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:07.397
Those belong in the evidence.

43
00:03:08.657 --> 00:03:13.477
Read them next, compare complete data sets, and test the rule rather than trusting

44
00:03:13.477 --> 00:03:13.917
the gauge.
